---
scope: docs
intent: >
  docs/ is the canonical home of the AIDE methodology. Every rule, convention,
  and doctrine that the server ships through its two delivery channels
  originates here as editable Markdown. Each doc owns one dimension of the
  methodology — the spec, the template, progressive disclosure, agent-readable
  code, the automated QA loop — and the doc set is small on purpose so an
  agent receiving the full methodology through one aide_init call can
  internalize it in a single read. Adding, editing, or removing a doc here
  is the only lever by which AIDE itself evolves.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - Exactly five canonical docs, one per dimension of the methodology:
      the spec, the template, progressive disclosure, agent-readable code,
      automated QA. Each doc owns its dimension completely; no two docs
      share a topic.
    - Every doc is written in decision-form, not description-form — the
      same standard `.aide` specs follow, because tool descriptions and
      methodology blocks are composed from these files verbatim and must
      teach an agent what to do and why.
    - Docs are standalone Markdown. Cross-references between docs in this
      folder use relative Markdown links (`./progressive-disclosure.md`);
      nothing links into a vault, a URL, or an absolute path the reader
      can't resolve after the doc ships to a host project.
    - A doc edit is the only way to change what downstream host projects
      receive. A maintainer who wants to introduce a new rule lands it in
      a doc here first; everything downstream composes from that edit.
    - The full doc set fits comfortably in one init's worth of reading.
      An agent that receives it through aide_init has the whole
      methodology loaded without further research, external docs, or
      brain lookups.
  undesired:
    - A sixth doc added without showing that none of the existing five
      can absorb the new content. The methodology must stay small enough
      to be received in one shot; unbounded doc growth defeats that.
    - Doctrine that lives in a README, a CLAUDE.md, a commit message, a
      code comment, or a tool description hand-written from memory. If a
      rule is not in a doc under this folder, it is not AIDE, even if
      everyone on the project believes it is.
    - Two docs overlapping on the same topic. If "cascading intent" ends
      up in both aide-spec.md and a new cascading-intent.md, the server's
      composer has to pick a winner and the next edit to one leaves the
      other stale. One dimension, one doc.
    - Vault-specific syntax leaking into a canonical doc: Obsidian
      wikilinks, `tags:` frontmatter, "Part of [[...]]" footers. These
      break the moment the doc ships out of this folder.
    - A doc rewritten in flowing prose "for readability" — description
      where a decision used to be. The reader of these files is the
      server's build pipeline and aide_init's runtime, not a human
      browsing a blog. Prose is paraphrasing in disguise.
    - A doc that describes behavior the server does not actually deliver.
      The canonical docs are operational truth, not aspirational
      planning; a doc that promises an unimplemented pipeline phase or
      tool is worse than a missing doc because it teaches a lie.
---

## Context

Before this folder existed, the methodology lived in a personal Obsidian
vault. Every new project had to rediscover the rules from a vault it
could not access, and any downstream copy (a CLAUDE.md snippet, a README
section) went stale the moment the vault notes changed. Migrating to
this folder is the move that makes the methodology shippable: one
source, two delivery channels, no paraphrasing at the edges.

The reader of these docs is not a human browsing. It is the server's
build pipeline (which composes tool descriptions from them) and
aide_init's runtime (which reads them to assemble the methodology block
it installs in host projects). Every sentence in this folder will end
up in one of those two places. Writing for that reader — the composer,
not the browser — is what keeps the voice tight and the content
actionable.

## Strategy

**One doc per dimension, zero overlap.** The five dimensions — spec,
template, progressive disclosure, agent-readable code, automated QA —
partition the methodology cleanly. Each doc owns its dimension
completely; if a topic fits two docs, one of them is wrong. Overlap
dilutes the doc set and forces the server's composer to pick a winner
arbitrarily, which is the failure mode this strategy exists to prevent.

**Decision-form, not description-form.** These docs are the source text
for tool descriptions and installed methodology blocks. Tool
descriptions must teach — they tell an agent what to do and why. So
the docs themselves must be written that way, because any prose shift
at composition time would be paraphrasing, which the parent spec
explicitly forbids. Every paragraph should state a choice and its
justification; every rule should be falsifiable.

**Standalone Markdown, no vault dependencies.** No Obsidian wikilinks,
no Obsidian-style frontmatter tags, no "Part of [[...]]" footers,
nothing that depends on software outside the repo to resolve. When
these files are installed into a host project by aide_init, they must
travel as self-contained Markdown that opens correctly in any editor.
Cross-references between docs in this folder use relative Markdown
links only.

**Edit-in-place is the forcing function.** A new rule enters AIDE by
landing in a doc here first. If a maintainer ever writes the rule
anywhere else first — a commit message, a tool description, a host
project's CLAUDE.md — the rule is not yet AIDE, and the discipline is
to move it into a doc before shipping. "Land it in docs/ or it doesn't
ship" is the single rule that keeps the source of truth singular.

**Small, not comprehensive.** The doc set aims to be complete, not
exhaustive. An agent receiving the full methodology through one
aide_init call must be able to internalize it in a single read. Adding
a sixth doc requires the same bar as adding a sixth tool: show that
none of the existing five can absorb the new content. Growth is a
regression until proven otherwise.

## Good examples

A maintainer discovers an edge case in cascading intent where two
sibling submodules have locally incompatible outcomes. They open
`aide-spec.md`, find the Cascading Intent section, and add a paragraph
explaining how the parent's outcomes resolve the conflict. They
commit. On the next rebuild, `aide_discover`'s tool description picks
up the updated text because it was composed from the same paragraph.
On the next `aide_init` run in a host project, the installed
methodology block contains the same updated text. One edit, both
channels, zero paraphrasing.

A contributor wants to add a rule about inline comments on helpers.
They scan the doc set looking for where it fits: `progressive-
disclosure.md` already covers inline narration under Tier 3
(orchestrators only, not helpers). The new rule is an expansion of the
existing discussion, so it lands there as a sharpened paragraph rather
than a new doc. The doc set stayed at five files, and the reader can
still find the rule on the first try.

An agent in a fresh host project runs `aide_init`. The installed
methodology block is a concatenation of the five canonical docs in a
stable order. The agent reads it once end-to-end and has the full
methodology loaded with no external references to chase — no brain, no
website, no "see the project README for details." The single-read
budget is honored.

## Bad examples

A contributor opens a PR adding `docs/cascading-intent.md` as a new
standalone doc. But cascading intent is already covered in
`aide-spec.md` under the Inheritance and Cascading Intent sections. Two
docs now own the same dimension; a future edit to one leaves the other
stale; the server's composer picks one arbitrarily and the other rots.
The fix is to land the content in the existing section, not to create
a new doc.

A doc opening with "Progressive disclosure is a beautiful idea rooted
in the principle that understanding should deepen on demand, not all
at once..." The decision that used to be "folder tree is Tier 1, JSDoc
is Tier 2, inline narration is Tier 3, helpers don't get Tier 3" has
been dissolved into mood. An agent composing a tool description from
this doc now has to extract the rules back out of the prose — which
is paraphrasing, which is what the parent forbids.

A doc that references a brain note with `See [[projects/aidemd-
mcp/automated-qa|Automated QA]]`. The wikilink points at a file the
reader (an agent in a host project, or the server's build pipeline)
cannot resolve. The doc has leaked its origin and will render as
broken text the moment it ships. Relative Markdown links to other
files in this same folder are the only acceptable form.

A new rule that lands only as a commit message: "aide_scaffold must
never overwrite existing files — new safety rule." The behavior gets
implemented in the server's source, tests pass, the commit merges.
But `aide-spec.md`'s writing standards section was never updated, so
when a contributor re-derives the spec six months later the rule is
gone. Git history is not canonical documentation.
